"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"grade\":\"partial\",\"rationale\":\"The answer correctly separates borrower default, collateral-shortfall and cash-reinvestment risk. It says cash-collateral reinvestment can lose money, even more than lending income. It keeps the US iShares example apart from UCITS rules. It also avoids promising that collateral eliminates losses, stating that controls 'mitigate risk; they do not eliminate it.' For UCITS it covers the ability to recall loans and the valuation, haircut and diversification requirements for collateral. There are substantive omissions, however. (1) For the iShares example, it does not state that collateral is maintained at least equal to the current market value of the securities lent. It lists only generic controls such as overcollateralization and haircuts, which are not tied to the iShares terms. (2) On the UCITS side, it omits the key reinvestment rules: noncash collateral cannot be reinvested, and cash reinvestment is restricted and must be diversified. Liquidity and credit-quality criteria for collateral are only loosely implied by 'eligibility.' (3) Its cash-reinvestment controls are generic and not tied to either regime. Because the central risk distinctions are right but the regime-specific collateral and reinvestment rules are incomplete, the grade is partial.\"}","ttft_ms":3165,"type":"result","duration_ms":6904,"uuid":"8a486d46-9493-45fb-bfb3-381576061030","ttft_stream_ms":528,"time_to_request_ms":53,"first_content_frame_ms":528,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
